  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q9-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Explain why the conjugate of $3-\\sqrt{4}$ is not $3+\\sqrt{4}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\sqrt{4}=2$, so $3-\\sqrt{4}=1$ is rational and its conjugate is itself, $1$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3-\\sqrt{4}&=3-2\\\\3-\\sqrt{4}&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThe result is rational, so its conjugate is $1$. In contrast, $3+\\sqrt{4}=5$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
